1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,879
I'm Mario Ritter with the VOA Special English Education Report. Some American

2
00:00:06,879 --> 00:00:11,800
parents might think their children need better educations to compete with China

3
00:00:11,800 --> 00:00:19,559
and other countries. But how much do the parents themselves need to change? A new

4
00:00:19,559 --> 00:00:26,519
book called Battle Him of the Tiger Mother by Amy Chua has caused a debate about

5
00:00:26,519 --> 00:00:34,119
cultural differences in parenting. Ms. Chua is a professor at the Yale Law School in

6
00:00:34,119 --> 00:00:40,719
New Haven, Connecticut and the mother of two daughters. She was raised in the

7
00:00:40,719 --> 00:00:47,879
American Midwest by immigrant Chinese parents. In the Chinese culture, the Tiger

8
00:00:47,879 --> 00:00:55,439
represents strength and power. In her book, Ms. Chua writes about how she demanded

9
00:00:55,439 --> 00:01:00,920
excellence from her daughters. For example, she threatened to burn her daughter's

10
00:01:00,920 --> 00:01:07,439
stuffed animals unless she played a piece of music perfectly. She would insult her

11
00:01:07,439 --> 00:01:15,000
daughters if they failed to meet her expectations. Ms. Chua says she had a clear

12
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,120
list of what her daughters, Sophia and Luisa, were not permitted to do. They could

13
00:01:22,120 --> 00:01:30,079
not attend a sleepover. Have a play date. Watch TV or play computer games. Be in a

14
00:01:30,079 --> 00:01:37,080
school play or get any grade less than an A. Many people have criticized Amy Chua.

15
00:01:37,080 --> 00:01:43,280
Some say her parenting methods were abusive. She even admits that her husband,

16
00:01:43,280 --> 00:01:50,360
who is not Chinese, sometimes objected to her parenting style. But she says that

17
00:01:50,359 --> 00:01:57,400
was the way her parents raised her and her three sisters. Ms. Chua makes fun of

18
00:01:57,400 --> 00:02:03,640
her own extreme style of parenting. She says she eased some of the pressure after her

19
00:02:03,640 --> 00:02:11,240
younger daughter rebelled and shouted, I hate my life, I hate you. Ms. Chua says

20
00:02:11,240 --> 00:02:16,800
she decided to retreat when it seemed like there was a risk that she might lose

21
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:24,960
her daughter. But she also says American parents often have low expectations of their children's

22
00:02:24,960 --> 00:02:33,600
abilities. She says one of the biggest differences I see between Western and Chinese parents

23
00:02:33,600 --> 00:02:41,600
is that Chinese parents assume strength rather than fragility. Stacey DeBroff has written

24
00:02:41,759 --> 00:02:49,199
four books on parenting. She says the debate over Ms. Chua's book has to do with two questions.

25
00:02:50,639 --> 00:02:56,799
What does it mean to be a successful parent? And what does it mean to be a successful child?

26
00:02:58,400 --> 00:03:05,919
Ms. DeBroff says Amy Chua's parenting style is not limited to Chinese families. She says it

27
00:03:05,920 --> 00:03:13,040
represents a traditional way of parenting among immigrants seeking a better future for their children.

28
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:22,640
But she also sees a risk. When children have no time to be social or to follow their own interests,

29
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:30,960
they might not develop other skills that they need to succeed in life. Stacey DeBroff advises

30
00:03:30,960 --> 00:03:37,520
parents to develop their own style of parenting and not just repeat the way they were raised.

31
00:03:38,480 --> 00:03:46,640
And that's the VOA Special English Education Report. What are your thoughts about parenting styles

32
00:03:46,640 --> 00:03:56,400
and cultural differences? Tell us at VOAspecialEnglish.com or on Facebook at VOA Learning English.

33
00:03:56,400 --> 00:04:00,879
For VOA Special English, I'm Mario Ritter.

